  "feed_brier": 0.157516,
  "feed_note": "the MLB feed's own wp_home_before, scored on the same plays, for comparison. Measured 2026-09-06 it is 0.157516 against this model's 0.155718, so the context-free model is very slightly BETTER on Brier despite knowing nothing about the teams. That is not a claim to have beaten MLB at win probability: the feed is a live in-game number solving a different problem, and the gap is small. What it does settle is that insisting on a team-blind valuation costs nothing measurable here, which is the only question this module needed answered. The feed is still not the engine, because its within-cell standard deviation of 0.04 means it carries team quality, and crediting a manager for having better players is the confound this module exists to avoid."
 },
 "why_a_model_not_a_table": "a table of cell means at this resolution cannot support differencing. Measured on the first build: advancing a runner from first to second LOWERED the batting team's win expectancy in 42.6% of comparable cells, because 1.45 million plays over 4,557 cells is about 318 each, the standard error of a binary outcome at that count is 0.029, and the effect being measured is 0.02 to 0.05. The feed's own numbers averaged the same way violate it in 24.6%.",
 "smoothing_costs_the_tails": "the model is less sharp than the raw cell means at the extremes. Bottom of the ninth tied, bases empty reads 0.6295 / 0.5868 / 0.5556 by outs against published values near 0.63 / 0.57 / 0.52: the leadoff cell matches and the two-out cell is about three points high. That is the price of a functional form smooth enough to difference, and it is a price worth paying, because a table that is right on average and non-monotone in the base state cannot value a decision at all. Any family reading the ninth-inning tail should say so.",
 "monotone_by_construction": "the base-out state enters ONLY through its run expectancy, as a single covariate with a positive coefficient, so advancing a runner always raises the batting team's win probability. That property is what makes differencing safe and it is asserted in the tests, not assumed.",
 "state_excludes": "the teams, the batter, the pitcher, the park and the count. Every one matters to a real win probability and every one would let a manager's roster leak into his grade. This is a league-average valuation of a SITUATION, which is the only thing a decision can be fairly scored against.",
 "extra_innings_note": "innings past the cap read as zero regulation half-innings remaining. Since 2020 extra innings begin with an automatic runner on second, so the tenth is a structurally different game; it is pooled rather than modelled."
}
